--TEST--
`Core\Server::isDraining` is one process-wide fact rather than a per-call one: swept over a hundred calls, every answer agrees with the first and the count says so — `rule:http-server/the-server-block-is-boot-class`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 5 puts the drain process-wide on purpose: a shutdown drains the process,
// so a bit answered from whichever core or whichever call took the question
// would report different things about one server. That is a property of the
// whole sweep and not of any line in it — a member that constructed its answer
// per call, or read a fact belonging to the caller rather than to the process,
// prints plausibly on every single line and fails only on the count.
bool $first = Core\Server::isDraining();

int $asked = 0;
int $agreed = 0;
int $i = 0;
for ($i = 0; $i < 100; $i = $i + 1) {
    bool $answer = Core\Server::isDraining();
    $asked = $asked + 1;
    if ($answer == $first) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
}

// Counted, not read off a line: `asked` and `agreed` are equal only if the
// answer never moved under a hundred readings of one unchanging process.
echo "asked: ", $asked, "\n";
echo "agreed: ", $agreed, "\n";

// The sweep would also pass if every answer were the *wrong* one consistently,
// so the value the run agreed on is named beside the count.
if ($first) {
    echo "agreed on: draining\n";
} else {
    echo "agreed on: accepting\n";
}
--EXPECT--
asked: 100
agreed: 100
agreed on: accepting
